---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import StoryFrame from '../components/StoryFrame.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';

const APP = 'https://atheer-next.fly.dev/';
const SB = 'https://atheer-next-storybook.fly.dev/';
const PLAN = 'https://atheer-306090.fly.dev/';

/** Status is carried by shape first, so it survives greyscale and small sizes. */
const shapes = [
  { key: 'square', label: 'Blocked' },
  { key: 'triangle', label: 'At risk' },
  { key: 'circle', label: 'On track' },
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Dense operational software, and you can open it right now"
  metaTitle="Operational software — live product and component library | Tyler Young"
  description="A working field-operations surface: status carried by shape rather than colour, and a sidebar that surfaces what changed instead of waiting to be clicked."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="02 · Data-Dense B2B"
  meta={[
    ['Live', `<a href="${APP}">atheer-next.fly.dev</a>`],
    ['Components', `<a href="${SB}">atheer-next-storybook.fly.dev</a>`],
    ['Persona', 'Monitors field work all day; arrives resuming, not browsing'],
    ['Built with', 'Paper↔Claude Code · React · Tailwind · Storybook'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      Both links above are running software, not screenshots. The product surface is
      one; the component library it was assembled from is the other. Everything
      below is a note on what to look at once you are in them.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Status is a shape before it is a colour</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Open the product and look at the sidebar and the job IDs. State is carried by
      a hollow shape — square, triangle, circle — not by a colour swatch. The same
      glyph appears on the nav item, the job header and the status column, so the
      blocked square you notice in the sidebar is the identical mark you find on the
      row that caused it.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      It survives greyscale, it works at eleven pixels, and it means colour is left
      free to do something else.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <figure class="fig shapes">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 372 92" role="img" aria-labelledby="shapes-title">
      <title id="shapes-title">
        Three hollow status glyphs: a square for blocked, a triangle for at risk,
        and a circle for on track
      </title>
      {
        shapes.map((s, i) => {
          const cx = 62 + i * 124;
          return (
            <g>
              {s.key === 'square' && <rect x={cx - 13} y="20" width="26" height="26" class="glyph" />}
              {s.key === 'triangle' && (
                <path d={`M ${cx} 18 L ${cx + 15} 46 L ${cx - 15} 46 Z`} class="glyph" />
              )}
              {s.key === 'circle' && <circle cx={cx} cy="33" r="14" class="glyph" />}
              <text x={cx} y="76" class="glyph-label">{s.label}</text>
            </g>
          );
        })
      }
    </svg>
  </figure>

  <h2>The sidebar carries live state</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Each application in the nav carries a live line underneath it — a certification
      expiring tomorrow, a safety step skipped on a numbered job, a rework rate up
      week over week. Navigation stops being a menu and becomes a set of threads out
      of today's problem, which means people find capabilities by working rather
      than by being onboarded to them.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <StoryFrame
    id="components-sidebar--home-active"
    label="Sidebar"
    source="Paper node IKC-1"
    height={712}
    note="Live from the component library. Configurable: active item, labels, subtitle text, status shapes, icons."
  />

  <h2>Components are earned, not scaffolded</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Every component in the library traces to a specific node in the design file —
      the inventory records which one, and what is configurable on each. Nothing was
      created speculatively. If a component had not appeared in a design, it did not
      belong in the library.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <StoryFrame
    id="components-equipmentcard--default"
    label="EquipmentCard"
    source="Paper node IRG-1"
    height={690}
    note="Title, spec values, section labels and link lists are all configurable; variants cover many links, no image, and two theme palettes."
  />

  <p>
    <Draft>
      The library also carries the token page — colours, typography, spacing, radii,
      borders and shadows — which is the constraint the whole system generates
      inside. It is the first thing to open if you want to know whether this was a
      system or a folder of components.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <p class="artifact-row">
    <a class="artifact" href={SB} data-live target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Open the component library
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
    <a class="artifact" href={APP} data-live target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Open the product
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>The plan was software too</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The engagement plan is an application the client makes a decision inside. They
      pick which surface to build first and which second; the choice persists
      server-side, so everyone opening the link sees the same plan, and the third
      phase takes whatever is left. It is still running on the selections stored
      against it — landing page first, context engine second.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Same instinct as the operating spec, pointed at people instead of an agent:
      put the constraint where the decision gets made. Seventy-three lines of Flask,
      a single-row table and a mounted volume so the choice survives a restart. What
      it commits to is a plan; what landed against it is the question below.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <p class="artifact-row">
    <a class="artifact" href={PLAN} data-live target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Open the 90-day plan
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>What shipped</h2>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      Tyler — which parts of this reached production and which stayed design. Not
      guessing at it.
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      The data in the live product is invented — the equipment, the sites and the
      people are not real. A second surface from the same engagement carries a named
      end customer and is deliberately not published.
    </p>
  </div>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-managed-ux-complexity',
        note: 'This is the complexity budget applied to one surface. Making friction measurable is what lets a team argue about density with evidence instead of taste, and catch scope bloat while it is still cheap to remove.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-scaled-design-efficiency',
        note: 'The same argument as the design system that came before it: teams move faster when the shared thing is assumed rather than optional. That one spread through usefulness rather than mandate — so does this one.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 36px !important;
  }

  .fig {
    margin: 0 0 34px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    background: var(--color-paper-raised);
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .fig svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 372px;
    height: auto;
  }

  .glyph {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-ink);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .glyph-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    fill: var(--color-ink-3);
    text-anchor: middle;
  }

  .artifact-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 14px 26px;
    margin: 30px 0 0 !important;
  }
</style>
